HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The iinnssttaallll utility appeared in 4.2BSD.

BBUUGGSS
     The meaning of the --MM option has changed as of FreeBSD 9.2 and it now
     takes an argument.  Command lines that used the old --MM will get an error
     or in rare cases will append logs to the first of multiple source files
     rather than installing it.

     Temporary files may be left in the target directory if iinnssttaallll exits ab‐
     normally.

     File flags cannot be set by fchflags(2) over a NFS file system.  Other
     file systems do not have a concept of flags.  The iinnssttaallll utility will
     only warn when flags could not be set on a file system that does not sup‐
     port them.

     The iinnssttaallll utility with --vv falsely says a file is copied when --CC snaps
     hard links.

